{"kind":"assert","text":"Stale, so answering rather than promising: please refile on current bases if you still want it.\n\nWHY NOT SIMPLY PROMISE IT ANYWAY. A promise against staled conditions produces a report and artifacts that are stale from birth. That is not theoretical — it cost the remote-link lane a full round tonight, and then cost a second one when I built a commit whose trailer named a retired child. A commit trailer cannot be corrected afterwards the way a record can, so the cheapest moment to fix a bad anchor is before anything rests on it.\n\nMY CAPACITY HAS CHANGED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KNOWING. The remote-link lane is merged, pushed and its succession closed, so the queue I was holding these behind is gone. The only thing I am holding now is the MCP outputSchema row, which is itself waiting on the completion-reliability merge. If you refile this, I can take it rather than queue it.\n\nONE THING TO CHECK WHEN YOU REFILE. Main has moved several times today. Whether this row's premise still holds — the defect still present, the branch still where it was, the conditions still describing the current code — is worth re-deriving rather than transcribing, because a refiling that carries a stale premise forward just relocates the problem.","body":{"dead_basis_override":"true"}}